1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:02,000
Let's get into model migration.

2
00:00:02,000 --> 00:00:06,000
Every time a model you depend on gets deprecated, you run into a question.

3
00:00:06,000 --> 00:00:10,000
How do you move to a new model without breaking what already exists?

4
00:00:10,000 --> 00:00:14,000
This lesson covers pinning to an exact model ID,

5
00:00:14,000 --> 00:00:16,000
treating migration as ongoing maintenance,

6
00:00:16,000 --> 00:00:19,000
and testing against a frozen set before you switch.

7
00:00:19,000 --> 00:00:21,000
Let's break it down.

8
00:00:21,000 --> 00:00:23,000
Let's ground three terms first.

9
00:00:23,000 --> 00:00:25,000
First, a model ID.

10
00:00:25,000 --> 00:00:27,000
That's the exact string that names a model,

11
00:00:27,000 --> 00:00:33,000
like Claude Opus 4.8, the pinned snapshot for Opus 4.8.

12
00:00:33,000 --> 00:00:36,000
It selects both the weights and the behavior you get back.

13
00:00:36,000 --> 00:00:38,000
Second, version pinning.

14
00:00:38,000 --> 00:00:44,000
That means naming an exact model ID in your app instead of a floating alias,

15
00:00:44,000 --> 00:00:48,000
so a new release can't silently change how your app behaves.

16
00:00:48,000 --> 00:00:50,000
Third, deprecation.

17
00:00:50,000 --> 00:00:55,000
That's Anthropic marking a model for retirement on a set date,

18
00:00:55,000 --> 00:00:59,000
after which calls to it start failing and you're forced to migrate.

19
00:00:59,000 --> 00:01:02,000
Model ID, version pinning, deprecation.

20
00:01:02,000 --> 00:01:06,000
Those three are the vocabulary of every migration decision,

21
00:01:06,000 --> 00:01:11,000
and the rest of this lesson shows how they fit together into a workflow.

22
00:01:11,000 --> 00:01:14,000
Here's the foundation that makes pinning work.

23
00:01:14,000 --> 00:01:19,000
On the 4.6 generation and later, a model ID is a fixed snapshot.

24
00:01:19,000 --> 00:01:23,000
Anthropic doesn't go back and update the weights under an existing ID.

25
00:01:23,000 --> 00:01:29,000
When a better model is ready, it ships under a brand new ID, never on top of the old one.

26
00:01:29,000 --> 00:01:33,000
So when you pin to an exact ID, like Opus 4.8 snapshot,

27
00:01:33,000 --> 00:01:37,000
you get the same model on every single call today and next month.

28
00:01:37,000 --> 00:01:39,000
That's the whole point.

29
00:01:39,000 --> 00:01:43,000
Your behavior only changes when you decide to change the ID yourself.

30
00:01:43,000 --> 00:01:47,000
Compare that to leaning on a moving string that always points at the newest release.

31
00:01:47,000 --> 00:01:52,000
There, a launch you didn't ask for can shift your results overnight.

32
00:01:53,000 --> 00:01:56,000
Let me put the two choices side by side.

33
00:01:56,000 --> 00:02:01,000
A pinned snapshot ID is a fixed identifier naming one frozen model.

34
00:02:01,000 --> 00:02:07,000
From the 4.6 generation on, the ID itself is dateless, like clawed Opus 4.8,

35
00:02:07,000 --> 00:02:11,000
while the pre-4.6 IDs carry a date.

36
00:02:11,000 --> 00:02:14,000
Either way, the weights behind it never change,

37
00:02:14,000 --> 00:02:18,000
so your app behaves identically until you deliberately migrate.

38
00:02:18,000 --> 00:02:21,000
That's what you want in production, predictability.

39
00:02:21,000 --> 00:02:24,000
A floating alias is a pre-4.6 moving string

40
00:02:24,000 --> 00:02:28,000
that resolves to whatever the latest snapshot happens to be.

41
00:02:28,000 --> 00:02:30,000
It's convenient for quick experiments

42
00:02:30,000 --> 00:02:34,000
because you always get the newest model with no code change.

43
00:02:34,000 --> 00:02:37,000
But that convenience is exactly the risk.

44
00:02:37,000 --> 00:02:40,000
A new release can shift behavior underneath you,

45
00:02:40,000 --> 00:02:44,000
with nothing in your codebase changing to explain it.

46
00:02:44,000 --> 00:02:47,000
So for anything real, pin the exact ID.

47
00:02:47,000 --> 00:02:50,000
Save the floating alias for throwaway scripts,

48
00:02:50,000 --> 00:02:53,000
or a surprise upgrade costs you nothing.

49
00:02:53,000 --> 00:02:58,000
Clawed uses two ID formats, and knowing them keeps you out of trouble.

50
00:02:58,000 --> 00:03:03,000
The 4.6 generation and everything after use a dateless format Clawed.

51
00:03:03,000 --> 00:03:07,000
Then the name, then the major version, like Clawed Opus 4.8.

52
00:03:07,000 --> 00:03:09,000
Here's the key point people miss.

53
00:03:09,000 --> 00:03:13,000
That dateless ID is itself the pinned snapshot.

54
00:03:13,000 --> 00:03:16,000
It isn't a moving latest pointer that quietly upgrades.

55
00:03:16,000 --> 00:03:18,000
Older models, before 4.6,

56
00:03:18,000 --> 00:03:22,000
use a dated format that ends in a year-month-day stamp,

57
00:03:22,000 --> 00:03:26,000
like Clawed Sonnet 4.5, then the date.

58
00:03:26,000 --> 00:03:30,000
For those, the trailing date is what marks the exact snapshot you're calling.

59
00:03:30,000 --> 00:03:32,000
Same idea both ways.

60
00:03:32,000 --> 00:03:37,000
The string names one fixed model, and that's what you pin against.

61
00:03:37,000 --> 00:03:40,000
Now, a wrinkle that trips people up.

62
00:03:40,000 --> 00:03:42,000
For those older dated models,

63
00:03:42,000 --> 00:03:47,000
the API also offered a shorter alias as a convenience,

64
00:03:47,000 --> 00:03:53,000
an alias with no date points at the most recent dated snapshot for that version.

65
00:03:53,000 --> 00:03:56,000
It's handy because you automatically pick up the freshest fix.

66
00:03:56,000 --> 00:03:58,000
But notice the trade.

67
00:03:58,000 --> 00:04:01,000
You've given up reproducibility since the model can move under you.

68
00:04:01,000 --> 00:04:03,000
Here's the part to remember.

69
00:04:03,000 --> 00:04:07,000
Those aliases are a pre-4.6 convenience only.

70
00:04:07,000 --> 00:04:10,000
They aren't offered for the newer dateless IDs.

71
00:04:10,000 --> 00:04:12,000
For a dateless ID, there's nothing to resolve,

72
00:04:12,000 --> 00:04:15,000
because the ID itself is already the pin.

73
00:04:15,000 --> 00:04:20,000
So don't go looking for a floating alias on the newer models.

74
00:04:20,000 --> 00:04:23,000
Here's the workflow, four steps in order.

75
00:04:23,000 --> 00:04:25,000
Step 1, pin the current model.

76
00:04:25,000 --> 00:04:28,000
Lock your app to the exact ID you run today,

77
00:04:28,000 --> 00:04:31,000
so nothing shifts while you prepare.

78
00:04:31,000 --> 00:04:33,000
Step 2, freeze an eval set.

79
00:04:33,000 --> 00:04:36,000
Gather a fixed set of representative prompts

80
00:04:36,000 --> 00:04:39,000
with the outputs you expect, and don't touch it.

81
00:04:39,000 --> 00:04:41,000
Step 3, test the new model.

82
00:04:41,000 --> 00:04:44,000
Run that frozen eval against the candidate model

83
00:04:44,000 --> 00:04:48,000
and compare the results side by side with the current one.

84
00:04:48,000 --> 00:04:50,000
Step 4, cut over deliberately.

85
00:04:50,000 --> 00:04:55,000
Once the new results hold up, switch the pinned ID in one reviewed change

86
00:04:55,000 --> 00:04:57,000
and watch it in production.

87
00:04:57,000 --> 00:05:01,000
Pin, freeze, test, cut over.

88
00:05:01,000 --> 00:05:06,000
Four steps that turn a scary migration into routine, low drama maintenance.

89
00:05:06,000 --> 00:05:09,000
I want to reframe how you think about migration,

90
00:05:09,000 --> 00:05:12,000
because the framing trips people up.

91
00:05:12,000 --> 00:05:16,000
Migrating models isn't a one-time task you finish during development.

92
00:05:16,000 --> 00:05:20,000
It's recurring maintenance work, and deprecation is what drives it.

93
00:05:20,000 --> 00:05:24,000
When Anthropic deprecates a model, it gets a retirement date,

94
00:05:24,000 --> 00:05:28,000
and after that date, calls to it, fail with a 404.

95
00:05:28,000 --> 00:05:31,000
An app that never migrated simply stops working.

96
00:05:31,000 --> 00:05:33,000
This isn't hypothetical.

97
00:05:33,000 --> 00:05:37,000
Opus 4.1 is already deprecated with a retirement date,

98
00:05:37,000 --> 00:05:43,000
and older models like Sonnet 3.7 and Haiku 3.5 have already been retired.

99
00:05:43,000 --> 00:05:46,000
So put model migration on your maintenance calendar,

100
00:05:46,000 --> 00:05:49,000
right next to dependency updates and security patches.

101
00:05:49,000 --> 00:05:52,000
Plan for it and its routine, ignore it,

102
00:05:52,000 --> 00:05:56,000
and a retirement date turns into an outage.

103
00:05:56,000 --> 00:05:59,000
Let's define the three ideas that make the testing step trustworthy.

104
00:05:59,000 --> 00:06:02,000
First, a frozen eval set.

105
00:06:02,000 --> 00:06:06,000
That's a fixed collection of prompts and expected outputs, held constant,

106
00:06:06,000 --> 00:06:11,000
so a current and a candidate model are judged on exactly the same inputs.

107
00:06:11,000 --> 00:06:14,000
If the inputs drift, the comparison means nothing.

108
00:06:14,000 --> 00:06:17,000
Second, a regression test.

109
00:06:17,000 --> 00:06:20,000
That's rerunning your eval against a new model

110
00:06:20,000 --> 00:06:23,000
to catch any behavior that changed for the worse,

111
00:06:23,000 --> 00:06:25,000
before it ever reaches a user.

112
00:06:25,000 --> 00:06:28,000
Third, prompt versioning.

113
00:06:28,000 --> 00:06:31,000
That's treating your prompts as versioned artifacts,

114
00:06:31,000 --> 00:06:34,000
tied to a model version and tracked in source control,

115
00:06:34,000 --> 00:06:38,000
so a change is reviewable and easy to roll back.

116
00:06:38,000 --> 00:06:43,000
Frozen eval set, regression test, prompt versioning.

117
00:06:43,000 --> 00:06:48,000
Together, they turn migration from a gut-feel decision into a measured one.

118
00:06:48,000 --> 00:06:50,000
Here's a subtle one.

119
00:06:50,000 --> 00:06:53,000
Some things change even when your call still succeeds,

120
00:06:53,000 --> 00:06:55,000
so a green light isn't the whole story.

121
00:06:55,000 --> 00:06:57,000
Start with tokens.

122
00:06:57,000 --> 00:07:00,000
The newer tokenizer on Opus 4.7 and later,

123
00:07:00,000 --> 00:07:03,000
plus Fable 5 and Sonnet 5,

124
00:07:03,000 --> 00:07:07,000
counts roughly 30% more tokens for the same text.

125
00:07:07,000 --> 00:07:10,000
That means your max output setting, your cost estimates,

126
00:07:10,000 --> 00:07:14,000
and your context budget can all be off after a switch,

127
00:07:14,000 --> 00:07:16,000
even though nothing erred.

128
00:07:16,000 --> 00:07:21,000
Another one, thinking display now defaults to omitted on the current models,

129
00:07:21,000 --> 00:07:23,000
so if your interface expected to show reasoning,

130
00:07:23,000 --> 00:07:25,000
it may suddenly look empty.

131
00:07:25,000 --> 00:07:30,000
So after you migrate, rebaseline your token counts and recheck your output.

132
00:07:30,000 --> 00:07:34,000
Don't assume the numbers you tuned on the old model still hold.

133
00:07:34,000 --> 00:07:37,000
One last nuance, and it's an important one.

134
00:07:37,000 --> 00:07:39,000
Pinning fixes the weights,

135
00:07:39,000 --> 00:07:42,000
but not everything that shapes a response lives in the weights.

136
00:07:42,000 --> 00:07:44,000
The serving infrastructure around the model,

137
00:07:44,000 --> 00:07:47,000
things like request routing and the safety classifiers,

138
00:07:47,000 --> 00:07:50,000
can change over time.

139
00:07:50,000 --> 00:07:53,000
That means you can see a shift in observable behavior,

140
00:07:53,000 --> 00:07:55,000
even on the exact same pinned ID.

141
00:07:55,000 --> 00:07:57,000
So pinning is powerful,

142
00:07:57,000 --> 00:08:01,000
but it isn't a guarantee that nothing will ever move.

143
00:08:01,000 --> 00:08:05,000
The practical answer is to keep that frozen eval set around

144
00:08:05,000 --> 00:08:07,000
and rerun it on a schedule,

145
00:08:07,000 --> 00:08:09,000
not only at migration time.

146
00:08:09,000 --> 00:08:12,000
Pinning removes the biggest source of surprise,

147
00:08:12,000 --> 00:08:14,000
the silent model swap.

148
00:08:14,000 --> 00:08:17,000
Periodic monitoring catches the smaller shifts

149
00:08:17,000 --> 00:08:19,000
that pinning alone can't.

150
00:08:19,000 --> 00:08:21,000
Use both together.

151
00:08:21,000 --> 00:08:23,000
Here's the bottom line.

152
00:08:23,000 --> 00:08:28,000
Pin an exact model ID so releases don't change behavior silently.

153
00:08:28,000 --> 00:08:32,000
Treat migration as recurring maintenance driven by deprecation.

154
00:08:32,000 --> 00:08:35,000
Test a new model on a frozen eval set first.

155
00:08:35,000 --> 00:08:38,000
Keep your prompts versioned in source control

156
00:08:38,000 --> 00:08:40,000
and rerun that eval on a schedule

157
00:08:40,000 --> 00:08:45,000
because pinning alone can't freeze everything around the model.

158
00:08:45,000 --> 00:08:48,000
And that wraps up migrating safely and version pinning.

159
00:08:48,000 --> 00:08:50,000
I'll see you in the next one.

